00:16:01.240 --> 00:16:03.430
none of us would be
here celebrating today.

00:16:03.430 --> 00:16:07.730
I ask the staff to stand if
they are not already standing,

00:16:07.730 --> 00:16:09.885
wherever they are, and be recognized.

00:16:09.885 --> 00:16:12.878
[APPLAUSE]

00:16:12.878 --> 00:16:18.862
I have left the most

00:16:18.862 --> 00:16:25.600
important for last.

00:16:27.020 --> 00:16:29.680
Each of you has been
supported by families and

00:16:29.680 --> 00:16:33.670
friends, as you will be
throughout your lives.

00:16:33.670 --> 00:16:38.730
They have provided encouragement,
validation, Love, and

00:16:38.730 --> 00:16:41.540
yes, in many cases,
financial support, too.

00:16:43.500 --> 00:16:47.360
While we will hand the diplomas to you,
you know that in

00:16:47.360 --> 00:16:52.100
parentheses after your name belong the
names of those who have nurtured you and

00:16:52.100 --> 00:16:54.290
supported you along the way.

00:16:54.290 --> 00:16:58.829
This is your opportunity to stand And
say thank you to your friends and

00:16:58.829 --> 00:17:03.224
family who are celebrating your
mutual accomplishment with you.

00:17:03.224 --> 00:17:06.568
[APPLAUSE]

00:17:06.568 --> 00:17:12.002
These days it is

00:17:12.002 --> 00:17:19.526
difficult to venture

00:17:19.526 --> 00:17:24.542
into the world

00:17:24.542 --> 00:17:29.976
of social media

00:17:29.976 --> 00:17:37.918
without encountering

00:17:37.918 --> 00:17:42.516
an article or

00:17:42.516 --> 00:17:48.786
discussion about

00:17:48.786 --> 00:17:57.982
whether Silicon Valley Is

00:17:57.982 --> 00:18:02.998
in the midst of

00:18:02.998 --> 00:18:09.280
another bubble.

00:18:11.140 --> 00:18:13.870
Whatever your opinion on that matter,

00:18:13.870 --> 00:18:19.045
it is hard to dispute that the GSB
is itself in a bit of a bubble.

00:18:19.045 --> 00:18:22.360
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It's a different kind of bubble,

00:18:22.360 --> 00:18:22.930
of course.

00:18:24.030 --> 00:18:28.360
It's a bubble in which you have
the space to learn and to explore.

00:18:28.360 --> 00:18:32.050
To discover incredible
classmates in distant countries.

00:18:32.050 --> 00:18:34.960
To dream about the future and
imagine your place within it.

00:18:35.970 --> 00:18:39.960
And not just to explore without,
but also within,

00:18:39.960 --> 00:18:44.280
on a journey of self-discovery.

00:18:44.280 --> 00:18:47.440
So it is a good bubble,
but a bubble nonetheless.

00:18:48.980 --> 00:18:54.200
Today, alas, as you walk across
the stage to receive your diplomas,

00:18:54.200 --> 00:18:58.950
you leave the bubble and
enter what during your time at the GSB,

00:18:58.950 --> 00:19:01.790
you have referred to as the real world.

00:19:03.680 --> 00:19:07.990
As I hand you your diploma and shake
your hand, en route to the real world,

00:19:07.990 --> 00:19:11.240
This is what I will be hoping for
you in the years ahead.

00:19:13.330 --> 00:19:18.300
First that you will take with
you the aspirational part of you

00:19:18.300 --> 00:19:21.680
that we have tried to nurture
during your time at the GSB.

00:19:22.930 --> 00:19:27.310
The cornerstone of the GSB reads,
dedicated to the things that

00:19:27.310 --> 00:19:31.030
haven't happened yet, And to the people
who are about to dream them up.

00:19:32.350 --> 00:19:33.460
You are those people.

00:19:34.610 --> 00:19:37.750
Dream big and hold on to those dreams.

00:19:37.750 --> 00:19:41.000
Change lives, organisations and the world.

00:19:43.200 --> 00:19:46.290
Second, that you will live your own life.

00:19:47.730 --> 00:19:50.160
You only have one, and
it is yours to live.

00:19:50.160 --> 00:19:56.710
You will encounter many people who will
have opinions about what is right for you.

00:19:56.710 --> 00:19:59.420
About the path you should follow.

00:19:59.420 --> 00:20:04.660
You'll be tempted by opportunities that
will look good rather than be good.

00:20:05.690 --> 00:20:11.170
Ask yourself, what you really want,
and have the courage to pursue it.

00:20:13.870 --> 00:20:18.620
Third, and this may seem odd coming
from a business school dean, but

00:20:18.620 --> 00:20:20.500
I hope you will keep your
career in perspective.

00:20:20.500 --> 00:20:25.780
There were many great viewpoints
in this year's view from the top.

00:20:25.780 --> 00:20:30.030
But I have to say, I agree in particular
with a point that Mitt Romney made.

00:20:31.130 --> 00:20:34.890
In the end we don't have that much
control over the world around us, and

00:20:34.890 --> 00:20:37.160
how it impacts our lives.

00:20:37.160 --> 00:20:41.520
The vagaries of the global economy and
politics will buffet you.

00:20:42.780 --> 00:20:46.710
But your true friends and
family will always be there for you, and

00:20:46.710 --> 00:20:49.060
that is what will make the difference.

00:20:49.060 --> 00:20:51.460
Always remember what is really important.

00:20:53.760 --> 00:20:58.610
Finally, I hope that you will stay
connected to and support one another.

00:20:59.810 --> 00:21:05.360
I have the privilege of meeting numerous
alumni across many graduating classes

00:21:05.360 --> 00:21:07.220
all over the world.

00:21:07.220 --> 00:21:10.990
I am always so pleased that they
remain bonded over time, and

00:21:10.990 --> 00:21:13.490
that they're always there for one another.

00:21:13.490 --> 00:21:17.560
Look to each other for confidence,
courage and strength, and

00:21:17.560 --> 00:21:19.110
stay connected to us as well.

00:21:22.450 --> 00:21:27.440
Our graduation speaker today is
an alumnus whose passion for business,

00:21:27.440 --> 00:21:32.810
social change, and family,
make him a quintessential GSBer.

00:21:34.310 --> 00:21:40.050
Tom Stayer graduated from
the Stanford GSB with an MBA in 1983.

00:21:40.050 --> 00:21:47.080
Tom was an Arjay Miller Scholar,
and I am so pleased that at age 99,

00:21:47.080 --> 00:21:51.530
Arjay is with us today to recognize
this year's Arjay Miller Scholars.

00:21:51.530 --> 00:21:53.045
Please join me in welcoming Arjay.

00:21:53.045 --> 00:22:02.182
[APPLAUSE]

00:22:02.182 --> 00:22:06.758
Back to

00:22:06.758 --> 00:22:13.617
Tom Steyer.

00:22:13.617 --> 00:22:18.240
Tom went on to Goldman Sachs,

00:22:18.240 --> 00:22:22.590
where he worked in the risk arbitrage
department under Robert Rubin.

00:22:22.590 --> 00:22:26.590
In January 1986,
he founded Farallon Capital Management,

00:22:26.590 --> 00:22:31.110
a pioneer in the practice of
absolute return investing,

00:22:31.110 --> 00:22:35.270
a strategy that aims to produce
a positive absolute return,

00:22:35.270 --> 00:22:40.700
regardless of the directions
of financial markets.

00:22:40.700 --> 00:22:43.990
In 2010, Tom announced that
he was turning his attention

00:22:43.990 --> 00:22:46.070
to having a broader social impact.

00:22:47.210 --> 00:22:51.590
He is actively engaged in climate politics
through his next gen climate political

00:22:51.590 --> 00:22:55.250
organization, and
works to promote economic development and

00:22:55.250 --> 00:22:59.330
environmental protection in California and
across the country.

00:23:00.580 --> 00:23:04.390
Tom is a former member of
the GSB's advisory council.

00:23:04.390 --> 00:23:07.310
And a current member of
the Stanford Board of Trustees.

00:23:08.890 --> 00:23:14.460
Together with his wife, Kat Taylor, who's
a Stanford JD MBA from the Class of 86,

00:23:14.460 --> 00:23:18.390
he was instrumental in the creation
of the joint business and

00:23:18.390 --> 00:23:23.150
law school, Steyer-Taylor Center for
Energy Policy and Finance, which

00:23:23.150 --> 00:23:27.970
examines issues of funding and public
policy surrounding alternative energy.

00:23:29.150 --> 00:23:32.113
Tom and
Kat also funded the TomKat Center for

00:23:32.113 --> 00:23:36.038
Sustainable Energy at
Stanford's Precourt Institute,

00:23:36.038 --> 00:23:41.324
which provides research grants for
alternative energy innovations in wind,

00:23:41.324 --> 00:23:44.703
solar and
advanced transmission technologies.

00:23:44.703 --> 00:23:48.292
Tom and Kat joined Warren Buffett,
Bill and Melinda Gates, and

00:23:48.292 --> 00:23:52.760
others in the giving pledge, a promise
to donate the majority of their wealth.

00:23:52.760 --> 00:23:57.480
To charitable and non-profit
activities during their lifetimes.

00:23:58.600 --> 00:24:03.160
They created and funded the Oakland-based
Beneficial State Bank and Foundation,

00:24:03.160 --> 00:24:07.770
which provides loans and banking services,
to under served small businesses,

00:24:07.770 --> 00:24:12.619
communities and individuals in California,
and along the west coast.

00:24:13.960 --> 00:24:16.060
It is a pleasure to have Tom with us.

00:24:16.060 --> 00:24:24.362
Please join me in giving a warm
GSB welcome to Tom Stire.

00:24:24.362 --> 00:24:34.345
[APPLAUSE]
&gt;&gt; Wow.

00:24:34.345 --> 00:24:37.490
[APPLAUSE] Well,
that was a very nice introduction, Garth.

00:24:37.490 --> 00:24:40.390
I wanna thank you all for having me here.

00:24:40.390 --> 00:24:43.950
And I know that you're all ready to get
out of here and celebrate with your

00:24:43.950 --> 00:24:48.850
families, because 32 years ago, I was
sitting exactly where you're sitting,

00:24:49.990 --> 00:24:54.940
and I know that a commencement speaker
gets one line if he or she is lucky.

00:24:56.090 --> 00:24:59.780
The best line from a commencement
speaker that I remember,

00:24:59.780 --> 00:25:03.170
dealt with what to remember
as you leave the house.

00:25:03.170 --> 00:25:05.045
Keys, wallet, phone.

00:25:05.045 --> 00:25:08.480
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; It's good advice,

00:25:08.480 --> 00:25:11.750
I use it every single day,
I used it this morning.

00:25:12.840 --> 00:25:15.210
But it only gets you out the door.

00:25:15.210 --> 00:25:19.680
And I'm hoping to talk today about what
happens after you leave the house.

00:25:19.680 --> 00:25:21.430
I'm not gonna give you my one line yet but

00:25:21.430 --> 00:25:26.640
I'm determined to come up with one line
that you guys can remember in 32 years.

00:25:26.640 --> 00:25:29.380
We'll see if it's as good as key,
wallet, phone.

00:25:29.380 --> 00:25:34.030
And let me say this, when I was
sitting where you're sitting now,

00:25:34.030 --> 00:25:40.520
I was excited to restart my career, but
I was very sorry to leave my friends,

00:25:40.520 --> 00:25:45.156
my woodside house, my unheated pool,
my girlfriend who is now my wife,

00:25:45.156 --> 00:25:49.970
and old Lahanda Road- to go live
in the office at Goldman Sachs.

00:25:51.270 --> 00:25:52.640
It was a very mixed bag.

00:25:53.960 --> 00:25:54.990
But let me say this.

00:25:56.880 --> 00:26:01.490
American business has real
structural advantages.

00:26:01.490 --> 00:26:06.830
As graduates of the GSB, you have
both the capability and the capacity

00:26:06.830 --> 00:26:11.740
to lead the world at scale and
those advantages are only gonna grow.

00:26:12.880 --> 00:26:16.990
In GSP terms, they are sustainable and
they are defensible.

00:26:18.210 --> 00:26:22.660
And as a society, we may be facing
enormous challenges, but while government

00:26:22.660 --> 00:26:27.255
gets to set the rules, it’s business
that can really push the envelope.

00:26:28.300 --> 00:26:33.050
And here’s why,
here’s what I think are unique and

00:26:33.050 --> 00:26:36.030
sustainable advantages that are in 2015.

00:26:36.030 --> 00:26:41.700
One, an awareness and dedication to
the power of information technology.

00:26:42.880 --> 00:26:47.630
And two, the ability to have a flexible
culture and incentive system.

00:26:48.730 --> 00:26:51.340
So let me just talk about just
those two points for a second.

00:26:52.620 --> 00:26:54.610
Information technology.

00:26:54.610 --> 00:26:59.100
A very smart investor said to me about
six months ago, that in his opinion,

00:26:59.100 --> 00:27:04.670
in every single business in the world,
the competitor that was the most IT

00:27:04.670 --> 00:27:08.950
enabled was the competitor that was gonna
come out on top over the next ten years.

00:27:08.950 --> 00:27:10.830
And that's every business.

00:27:10.830 --> 00:27:12.520
He was talking about farming, for

00:27:12.520 --> 00:27:16.080
instance, that the farmers with
the best IT were gonna win.

00:27:18.080 --> 00:27:23.800
The private sector is free to invest
in the information technology it needs.

00:27:23.800 --> 00:27:29.050
It's a huge advantage and it becomes,
itself, the cutting edge.

00:27:29.050 --> 00:27:33.650
My wife, who as Garth pointed out,
and I'm a humble MBA.

00:27:33.650 --> 00:27:35.635
My wife is a snotty JD MBA.

00:27:35.635 --> 00:27:39.410
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH]
&gt;&gt; She and I started a community bank,

00:27:39.410 --> 00:27:44.640
and it has about over $500 million
worth of assets, but we've tried

00:27:44.640 --> 00:27:49.160
to develop proprietary credit scoring IT
&gt;&gt; To compete

00:27:49.160 --> 00:27:53.420
to get the lowest default rates and
provide the best pricing and service.

00:27:53.420 --> 00:27:57.770
With the right technology,
we think we can outperform banks with over

00:27:57.770 --> 00:28:01.780
two trillion dollars worth of assets.

00:28:01.780 --> 00:28:03.660
In 2010, President Obama said,

00:28:03.660 --> 00:28:09.070
Washington lags a generation
behind in how we do business.

00:28:09.070 --> 00:28:11.010
With all due respect for

00:28:11.010 --> 00:28:13.858
the President of the United States,
I don’t think it’s one generation.

00:28:13.858 --> 00:28:19.090
Think about healthcare.gov.

00:28:19.090 --> 00:28:24.780
The Patent Office receives over 80%
of its applications electronically.

00:28:24.780 --> 00:28:27.470
It then prints them out, scans them, and

00:28:27.470 --> 00:28:30.520
enters them by hand Into
a case management system.

00:28:31.600 --> 00:28:33.450
Average processing time, three years.

00:28:35.840 --> 00:28:39.420
Every federal agency is depending
on Congress for funding, and

00:28:39.420 --> 00:28:42.310
Congress is not looking
to increase tech funding.

00:28:42.310 --> 00:28:45.540
They're not even looking to
support necessary upgrades.

00:28:45.540 --> 00:28:50.200
So where as government is large and
unwieldy, you can be nimble, innovative,

00:28:50.200 --> 00:28:51.330
and creative.

00:28:51.330 --> 00:28:52.640
This university,

00:28:52.640 --> 00:28:57.570
this valley are testimonies to
the really power of creative IT.

00:28:59.300 --> 00:29:03.930
Culture, the second point,
what you do to define your mission

00:29:05.310 --> 00:29:11.060
and how that mission informs every hiring
decision, every compensation decision,

00:29:11.060 --> 00:29:15.590
and every strategic decision means you
can not only get the best people, but

00:29:15.590 --> 00:29:17.990
you can organize them effectively.

00:29:17.990 --> 00:29:22.555
The private sector has a unique
ability to set agreed upon goals and

00:29:22.555 --> 00:29:24.980
work seamlessly to achieve them.

00:29:24.980 --> 00:29:28.780
By having a mission and setting your
incentives based on achieving that

00:29:28.780 --> 00:29:32.570
mission, you have an opportunity
to create an incredible impact.

00:29:34.120 --> 00:29:38.340
A very cynical friend of mine
told me 35 years ago, and

00:29:38.340 --> 00:29:41.580
I think this is really only
a part of what I'm talking about.

00:29:41.580 --> 00:29:44.710
But his statement was,
people do what you pay them to do.

00:29:46.550 --> 00:29:51.680
I watched the impact of different
incentive schemes at Farallon Management,

00:29:51.680 --> 00:29:56.286
which was the investment management
firm that I founded, over the years.

00:29:56.286 --> 00:29:59.240
And the impact was far
beyond what I imagined, so

00:29:59.240 --> 00:30:03.240
I know how powerful personnel
compensation can be.

00:30:04.480 --> 00:30:08.310
But Stanford's mission is about more
than gaining the highest gap return.

00:30:09.570 --> 00:30:11.740
Garth talked about the motto.

00:30:11.740 --> 00:30:16.590
Change lives, change organizations,
change the world.

00:30:16.590 --> 00:30:18.470
Sounds like unlimited upsides.

00:30:19.480 --> 00:30:20.330
What could go wrong?

00:30:21.630 --> 00:30:26.720
So, the question of the day is- how do
smart people, with all the advantages,

00:30:26.720 --> 00:30:27.530
make dumb decisions?

00:30:28.790 --> 00:30:30.120
It's not by being wrong.

00:30:31.445 --> 00:30:34.110
Because we're talking about smart
people who do good analysis.

00:30:35.220 --> 00:30:40.240
The way smart people make big mistakes
is by using the wrong framework.

00:30:40.240 --> 00:30:42.300
It's by looking too narrowly at a problem.

00:30:43.480 --> 00:30:47.470
You can be too limited either in
terms of time or in terms of scope,

00:30:47.470 --> 00:30:53.320
in general if you wanna solve
a thorny problem, make it bigger.

00:30:53.320 --> 00:30:57.840
Take information from outside any
one circumscribed discipline.

00:30:57.840 --> 00:31:01.580
So this is my one line of the day,
the one thing I want you to remember.

00:31:01.580 --> 00:31:02.500
It's two words.

00:31:03.630 --> 00:31:04.400
Be open.

00:31:05.530 --> 00:31:08.500
Widen the aperture on your camera.

00:31:08.500 --> 00:31:09.940
Use your peripheral vision.

00:31:11.040 --> 00:31:13.640
Be open to different
sources of information.

00:31:13.640 --> 00:31:15.820
It can be stressful to do
that in the short term,

00:31:15.820 --> 00:31:17.460
it can seem like a waste of time.

00:31:18.540 --> 00:31:22.320
But using a wide lens spurs creativity and

00:31:22.320 --> 00:31:27.280
collaboration, it makes you more flexible
and it respects all stakeholders.

00:31:28.830 --> 00:31:31.680
Let's take a quick look at
just two examples of what

00:31:31.680 --> 00:31:35.275
I think is the challenge of our
generation, carbon pollution.

00:31:38.282 --> 00:31:42.998
For decades, the Chinese government
focused exclusively on the GDP growth

00:31:42.998 --> 00:31:45.733
numbers in making their energy decisions.

00:31:45.733 --> 00:31:48.661
If you’re just running
the traditional accounting numbers,

00:31:48.661 --> 00:31:49.936
coal makes the most sense.

00:31:49.936 --> 00:31:52.281
It’s cheap on those numbers.

00:31:52.281 --> 00:31:56.820
And if you're trying to raise hundreds
of millions of people out of poverty and

00:31:56.820 --> 00:31:58.195
into the middle class,

00:31:58.195 --> 00:32:02.550
why wouldn't you wanna use the cheapest
source of energy to grow your GDP?

00:32:04.030 --> 00:32:08.360
That was the single bottom line for
the Chinese government for decades, but

00:32:08.360 --> 00:32:13.780
their GDP calculations didn't include any
cost for health and environmental impacts.

00:32:15.020 --> 00:32:17.680
And just because it doesn't
appear on your spreadsheet

00:32:18.700 --> 00:32:22.060
doesn't mean it doesn't
exist in the real world.

00:32:22.060 --> 00:32:27.150
Coal still accounts for more than 80% of
China's energy generation, and breathing

00:32:27.150 --> 00:32:30.910
the air in Beijing is the equivalent of
smoking two packs of cigarettes a day.

00:32:32.290 --> 00:32:37.980
Not recognizing and quantifying costs
does not mean those costs don't matter,

00:32:37.980 --> 00:32:40.480
they just don't matter
in your decision making.

00:32:40.480 --> 00:32:43.420
China has changed, now it computes and

00:32:43.420 --> 00:32:48.530
observes two bottom lines,
GDP growth and pollution.

00:32:48.530 --> 00:32:52.970
As a friend of mine says, if you don't
measure it, you can't manage it.

00:32:54.880 --> 00:32:58.790
So let's look for
an example of openness, and lo and

00:32:58.790 --> 00:33:02.830
behold it's the Stanford energy project.

00:33:02.830 --> 00:33:07.926
Stanford had to redo its heat and
electricity systems over the past

00:33:07.926 --> 00:33:12.756
two years, led by the real estate
department under Bob Reidy.

00:33:12.756 --> 00:33:16.020
How mundane, you say.

00:33:16.020 --> 00:33:17.350
Not really mundane.

00:33:18.710 --> 00:33:23.310
Before the decision was finalized, the
real estate department met with a group of

00:33:23.310 --> 00:33:27.850
graduate students, including my son,
who worried that Stanford wasn't

00:33:27.850 --> 00:33:32.210
taking a broad enough view of what
they were trying to accomplish.

00:33:32.210 --> 00:33:36.560
And after the meeting, I asked my
son how it went and he said, well,

00:33:36.560 --> 00:33:41.600
we didn’t change their minds in any way,
but they certainly changed mine.

00:33:42.630 --> 00:33:45.740
They knew way more about
the energy system than we did, and

00:33:45.740 --> 00:33:49.980
they were incorporating every concern
we had into their decision making.

00:33:51.010 --> 00:33:55.130
And I asked Bob what he thought of
the meeting, and he said, wow, they asked

00:33:55.130 --> 00:34:00.600
great questions, they love Stanford,
they care about the same things we do.

00:34:00.600 --> 00:34:03.640
Being open really went both ways.

00:34:03.640 --> 00:34:06.780
Bob did a great job of
opening up his aperture and

00:34:06.780 --> 00:34:10.180
thinking about what he was trying
to accomplish in a broad sense.

00:34:10.180 --> 00:34:14.355
And as a result, Stanford is gonna save
a lot of energy and a lot of water.

00:34:14.355 --> 00:34:18.640
We're gonna have 65% renewables by 2016,

00:34:18.640 --> 00:34:21.270
and we're gonna save a lot
of money along the way.

00:34:22.430 --> 00:34:23.760
More than any other school,

00:34:25.030 --> 00:34:28.830
Stanford University has the concept
of openness in its DNA.

00:34:29.890 --> 00:34:33.560
We’re blessed with the weather and

00:34:33.560 --> 00:34:37.710
a campus that makes collaboration and
collegiality easy.

00:34:38.790 --> 00:34:43.480
We spring from a West Coast culture
of friendliness and cooperation, and

00:34:43.480 --> 00:34:47.970
the university has deliberately sought to
foster cross-disciplinary problem solving.

00:34:49.920 --> 00:34:53.470
The GSB is thought to be the best
business school in the world,

00:34:53.470 --> 00:34:57.920
so you're starting from a place
of immense possibility.

00:34:57.920 --> 00:35:01.640
You're leaving with all
the tools to make a lasting,

00:35:01.640 --> 00:35:05.830
tremendous difference on this world,
but it cannot be all about you.

00:35:07.220 --> 00:35:10.630
You are the heirs to
an extraordinary system and

00:35:10.630 --> 00:35:14.720
the extraordinary competitive advantage
I was describing, just as I was.

00:35:17.470 --> 00:35:20.550
I think we all should be
really grateful for that.

00:35:20.550 --> 00:35:23.230
We should use the advantages
that American society and

00:35:23.230 --> 00:35:26.920
American business gives us,
because people have sacrificed for

00:35:26.920 --> 00:35:30.789
hundreds of years to pave
the road we stand on today.

00:35:31.860 --> 00:35:35.360
I hope you succeed magnificently, and

00:35:35.360 --> 00:35:39.760
I hope you share your success
with the rest of the world.

00:35:39.760 --> 00:35:46.030
Frankly, I'm expecting some millionaires,
and some billionaires, and

00:35:46.030 --> 00:35:52.770
some very talented very hardworking people
of modest means, to come out of this class

00:35:52.770 --> 00:35:57.270
and that's all great, that can't be
the measure of your accomplishment though.

00:35:58.320 --> 00:36:02.280
Take a wide view when
solving your problems, and

00:36:02.280 --> 00:36:05.540
an even bigger view when
defining your success.

00:36:06.970 --> 00:36:11.070
That will make everything you do fun and
constructive.

00:36:11.070 --> 00:36:15.070
I love my business career and
now I'm loving my next career.

00:36:16.610 --> 00:36:23.212
My two words, be open to the whole world,
please, and God bless you.

00:36:23.212 --> 00:36:23.743
Thank you.

00:36:23.743 --> 00:36:33.743
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:36:42.270 --> 00:36:45.095
&gt;&gt; It is now my great honor and

00:36:45.095 --> 00:36:51.522
pleasure to introduce you to
the GSB PhD class of 2015.

00:36:51.522 --> 00:36:55.415
Our PhD program is small and exclusive,

00:36:55.415 --> 00:36:59.202
with an admissions rate of under 7%.

00:36:59.202 --> 00:37:03.701
These individuals come here as
students dedicating themselves to

00:37:03.701 --> 00:37:05.307
years of course work and

00:37:05.307 --> 00:37:10.619
an intensive apprenticeship in conducting
research in their chosen fields.

00:37:11.690 --> 00:37:15.630
As a sign, consider that the students
who are graduating today

00:37:15.630 --> 00:37:18.240
began their careers in
the old South Building.

00:37:20.510 --> 00:37:25.120
Having become true experts,
they leave us as colleagues,

00:37:25.120 --> 00:37:27.600
setting out on a path to
create new knowledge.

00:37:28.690 --> 00:37:32.770
Today, we formally celebrate
their transition from students

00:37:32.770 --> 00:37:36.740
to future intellectual leaders, who will
play an important role in shaping our

00:37:36.740 --> 00:37:38.830
understanding of business and management.

00:37:40.220 --> 00:37:44.280
After their advisors place the hood of
the Stanford Business School on them,

00:37:44.280 --> 00:37:50.890
they will join us on stage as we welcome
them into our profession as colleagues.

00:37:50.890 --> 00:37:55.818
Dianne Le, Executive Director of the PHD
Program, will now read their names and

00:37:55.818 --> 00:37:58.584
their dissertation advisor will hood them.

